-- allium: 3
--
-- JEBOL: running scripts inside a host application.
--
-- This is what the project is for. Not interoperability, which is a poor
-- reason to build a REBOL: plenty of things call Java already. The reason
-- is running REBOL where production code already runs, with the operational
-- benefits of the JVM, and that means a host has to be able to hand a
-- script to an interpreter under bounds it sets and get a value back.
--
-- Three bounds matter, and none of them is optional in a web application:
-- how long a script may run, how deep it may nest, and what it may reach.
-- A script that cannot be stopped is a request that cannot be timed out.

use "./values.allium" as values

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Why a script stopped. The first is the ordinary case.
enum Conclusion {
    produced_a_value    -- the script finished and its value is available
    | raised            -- the script failed and the error is available
    | timed_out         -- the deadline passed before the script finished
    | cancelled         -- the host asked for it to stop
    | nested_too_deep   -- the script exceeded the nesting the host allowed
    | quit_early        -- the script itself asked to stop, and may say with what
}

-- What a script is allowed to reach outside itself.
enum HostAccess {
    none_at_all         -- the script sees only REBOL
    | reading           -- the script may read host values it was handed
    | reading_and_calling  -- the script may also call host methods
}

-- The kinds of thing outside the interpreter that a script can ask for.
-- Named as kinds rather than as single functions because a host grants or
-- refuses a kind: a host that lets a script read a file almost always
-- lets it read every file it can see, and a host that does not want the
-- file system does not want one corner of it either.
enum HostService {
    files               -- read, write, delete, rename, list, ask about
    | working_directory -- which directory a relative path counts from
    | environment       -- the names and values the host was started with
    | processes         -- start another program and wait for it
    | console           -- read a line from the operator, write one back
    | clock             -- what the time is now
    | network           -- open a connection to another machine
    | windows           -- ask the operator with a window on a screen
}

-- What feeds a started program's standard input.
--
-- The difference between the last two is the one that matters. A child given
-- nothing reads end of input at once and gets on with its work; a child that
-- inherited the host's input waits for whoever is on the other end of it,
-- who may never type anything. A script that meant the first and got the
-- second has hung, and nothing in the answer says why.
enum ProgramInput {
    the_hosts_own       -- the child reads from wherever the host reads
    | supplied_bytes    -- these bytes, and then end of input
    | a_files_contents  -- the named file, from its start to its end
    | nothing_at_all    -- closed, so the child reads end of input at once
}

-- Where a started program's standard output or standard error goes. The two
-- streams are directed separately and by the same four choices.
enum ProgramOutput {
    the_hosts_own       -- the child writes where the host writes
    | captured          -- the bytes come back to the interpreter
    | into_a_file       -- the bytes are written to the named file
    | discarded         -- the bytes are thrown away
}

-- Why a host service is not available. A script that asks for one it
-- cannot have must be able to tell these apart: the first two are the
-- host's decision and the third never changes.
enum ServiceRefusal {
    not_granted         -- the host has this service and did not grant it
    | not_present       -- the host itself has no such service to grant
    | never_portable    -- no host can offer it, so JEBOL never will
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What a host decides before a script runs. Every field is a bound, and
-- every bound has a default, because a host that must configure five
-- things before running anything will configure none of them.
value Bounds {
    wall_clock_limit: Duration
    maximum_nesting: Integer
    host_access: HostAccess
    -- Empty by default. A host that says nothing gets a script that can
    -- compute and cannot reach out, thus a host must name each service it
    -- wants, one at a time. The other bounds have useful defaults because
    -- a host that must set five things sets none of them; this one has no
    -- useful default, because the useful value is the one nobody guessed.
    granted_services: Set<HostService> = empty
}

-- What the host can tell the interpreter about one thing on its filesystem.
--
-- Six facts and no more, because this is the whole of what QUERY answers
-- and a port that offered more would be offering something no script can
-- ask for. Every field is optional except the name and the kind: a host
-- may know a file exists without being able to say when it was last read,
-- and a script asking for a fact the host has not got gets none rather
-- than a refusal. The refusal is for the service, not for the field.
value FileInformation {
    name: String                -- the path, as the interpreter spells it
    is_directory: Boolean       -- what QUERY's `type` field reports
    size: Integer?              -- none for a directory, and for a host that cannot say
    modified: Timestamp?
    accessed: Timestamp?
    created: Timestamp?
}

-- What crosses the boundary when a script starts another program.
--
-- The command arrives already separated, one entry for the program and one
-- for each argument, so the host never has to decide where an argument ends.
-- Quoting is the reason: a host handed one line has to split it, splitting
-- needs quoting rules, and the quoting rules differ between systems. A
-- script that wants the system's own rules asks for them, and that is what
-- the shell flag is.
value ProgramToStart {
    command: List<String>

    -- Whether the shell reads the command rather than the host starting the
    -- program itself. The shell does the splitting, the globbing and the
    -- redirecting.
    --
    -- The first entry is the command line the shell reads, and any entries
    -- after it become the shell's positional parameters: the second is $0,
    -- the third is $1, and so on. So a block command with the shell is not a
    -- list of arguments to the program, which is what it is without the
    -- shell, and reading it as one gives a command line that ignores
    -- everything after the first entry.
    --
    -- The single-entry case is the string command, where there was never
    -- more than one thing to send.
    read_by_the_shell: Boolean

    -- Whether the child is put on the host's own console. Asked for
    -- separately from inheriting the streams, because a host may have
    -- streams and no console to attach to.
    attached_to_the_hosts_console: Boolean

    -- Whether the interpreter waits for the child to exit before carrying
    -- on. It has to when anything is being captured, because there is
    -- nothing to hand back until the child has finished writing.
    waited_for: Boolean

    standard_input: ProgramInput
    input_bytes: List<Integer>?     -- when the input is supplied_bytes
    input_file: String?             -- when the input is a_files_contents

    standard_output: ProgramOutput
    output_file: String?            -- when the output goes into_a_file

    standard_error: ProgramOutput
    error_file: String?             -- when the error stream goes into_a_file
}

-- What the host tells the interpreter about a program it started.
--
-- The last field is the one to get right. A system that would not start the
-- program at all is a different outcome from a program that started and
-- failed, and the two are easy to conflate because both end with a script
-- that did not get what it wanted. An exit code says the program ran.
value ProgramResult {
    process_number: Integer
    exit_code: Integer?             -- only when the child was waited for
    captured_output: List<Integer>?
    captured_error: List<Integer>?
    refusal_message: String?        -- what the system said, when it refused
}

-- What a script asks for when it asks the operator to choose a file.
--
-- All five are optional and the dialog is usable with none of them, which is
-- why they are fields on a request rather than five separate ways of asking.
value FileChoiceRequest {
    for_saving: Boolean         -- choosing a name to write, not one to read
    several_at_once: Boolean    -- the operator may pick more than one
    starting_at: String?        -- the name or directory to open on
    window_title: String?       -- what the window's bar says

    -- What the chooser will show, flat: a name, then the pattern it stands
    -- for, then the next name, and so on. Empty when the script named none.
    --
    -- The two entries of a pair do different jobs. The name is what the
    -- operator reads in the chooser's menu and the pattern is what it selects
    -- by, so "Rebol scripts" then "*.r3" reads as a menu entry a person can
    -- pick, and a chooser offering "*.r3" as the name is one nobody can use.
    --
    -- Flat rather than already paired, because flat is what the script hands
    -- over and the pairing is a reading of it rather than a property of it. A
    -- list of an odd length has no pairing at all and is refused; a model that
    -- had paired them already could not say what was wrong with a list that
    -- would not pair.
    --
    -- Every entry is text by the time it crosses, whatever the script wrote.
    filters: List<String>
}

-- What a script may ask the operator for through a window on a screen.
--
-- Five things, and they are one service rather than five because a host that
-- will put one dialog on the screen will put any of them there. A grant per
-- dialog would let a host say which verb and not which screen, and the verb
-- was never the interesting half.
--
-- Every one of them answers none when the operator declines. That is not a
-- refusal and must not be reported as one: a person who closes a file
-- chooser has answered the question, and a script that cannot tell "no file"
-- from "no permission" will retry the wrong one.
enum WindowRequest {
    a_browser_at         -- open a URL or a local file in a web browser
    | a_file             -- choose a file, to read or to save, from a
                         -- FileChoiceRequest saying which files to offer
    | a_directory        -- choose a directory
    | a_colour           -- choose a colour
    | a_password         -- type a secret, without it appearing on screen
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One interpreter, owned by one thread, holding everything reachable from
-- it. A host running many scripts at once runs many of these; it does not
-- share one, because series share mutable storage and confining that to a
-- single thread is what makes it need no synchronisation.
entity Interpreter {
    bounds: Bounds
    user_context: values/Context

    runs: ScriptRun with interpreter = this
    active_runs: runs where status = running

    invariant OneScriptAtATime {
        active_runs.count <= 1
    }
}

entity ScriptRun {
    interpreter: Interpreter
    source_text: String
    started_at: Timestamp

    status: running | finished

    conclusion: Conclusion when status = finished
    result: values/Value when status = finished
    elapsed: Duration when status = finished

    deadline: started_at + interpreter.bounds.wall_clock_limit
    overdue: now >= deadline

    -- Which host service the script asked for and did not get, and why.
    -- Kept on the run and not only in the error value because a host that
    -- granted too little wants to see that from the outside, without
    -- reading the error the script saw.
    refused_service: HostService when conclusion = raised
    refusal_reason: ServiceRefusal when conclusion = raised

    -- Set when the host asked for the run to stop. The evaluator notices at
    -- its own convenience rather than being interrupted where it stands,
    -- because a thread stopped mid-mutation leaves values half-changed.
    cancellation_requested: Boolean

    -- Set when the script itself asked to stop, by calling QUIT. Kept
    -- apart from cancellation because the two are not the same event: one
    -- is the host overruling the script, the other is the script finishing
    -- early on purpose, and a host wants to tell them apart.
    quit_requested: Boolean
    quit_value: values/Value when quit_requested

    transitions status {
        running -> finished
        terminal: finished
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- Long enough that ordinary work finishes, short enough that a runaway
    -- script does not hold a request open until something else times out.
    default_wall_clock_limit: Duration = 5.seconds
    default_maximum_nesting: Integer = 10_000
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A script that asks for a host service it has not been granted gets an
-- error, and the error says which of the three reasons applies. Silence
-- is the failure this prevents: a READ that quietly answers none reads as
-- an empty file, and a script cannot tell the two apart.
rule RefusedServiceRaises {
    when: script_asks_for(run, service)
    requires: service not in run.interpreter.bounds.granted_services
    ensures:
        run.status = finished
        run.conclusion = raised
        run.refusal_reason in ServiceRefusal
        run.refused_service = service
}

-- Four things exist to call code written in C, thus no host can offer
-- them and no grant can turn them on. They are refused before the grant
-- is even looked at, and the reason says so.
rule ExtensionServicesAreNeverAvailable {
    when: script_asks_for(run, service)
    requires: service.is_an_extension_point
    ensures:
        run.status = finished
        run.conclusion = raised
        run.refusal_reason = never_portable
}

-- The current directory belongs to the interpreter and not to the process
-- that holds it. A host runs many interpreters at once and each one must
-- be able to sit in a different directory, thus changing one must not
-- move another. Every relative path is made absolute against the
-- interpreter's own directory before it reaches the host.
rule WorkingDirectoryIsPerInterpreter {
    when: script_changes_directory(run, path)
    requires: working_directory in run.interpreter.bounds.granted_services
    ensures:
        run.interpreter.working_directory = path
        for other in interpreters where other != run.interpreter:
            other.working_directory unchanged
}

-- A script asking about a file it cannot see gets none rather than a
-- refusal, because "there is nothing there" is an answer and a script has
-- to be able to act on it. The refusal is for the service: a script not
-- granted `files` cannot ask at all, and that raises.
rule AskingAboutAMissingFileAnswersNone {
    when: script_queries_file(run, path)
    requires: files in run.interpreter.bounds.granted_services
    requires: not host_has_anything_at(path)
    ensures: QueryResult(value: none)
}

-- A fact the host cannot supply is none for that field alone, and the rest
-- of the answer stands. A host that knows a file's size and not when it was
-- last read is an ordinary host, not a broken one.
rule AFactTheHostHasNotGotIsNoneOnItsOwn {
    when: script_queries_file(run, path, field)
    requires: files in run.interpreter.bounds.granted_services
    requires: host_has_anything_at(path)
    requires: information_of(path).field_named(field) = null
    ensures: QueryResult(value: none)
}

-- Starting another program hands the host a command already separated and a
-- decision about each of the child's three streams. Nothing about the shape
-- of the call is left for the host to work out.
--
-- Each stream is decided independently, which is what lets a script capture
-- what a program printed while letting its complaints go to the console the
-- operator is watching.
rule StartingAProgramCrossesAsACommandAndThreeStreams {
    when: script_starts_a_program(run, program)
    requires: processes in run.interpreter.bounds.granted_services

    ensures:
        program.command.count >= 1
        program.standard_input in ProgramInput
        program.standard_output in ProgramOutput
        program.standard_error in ProgramOutput
}

-- A capture waits, and the host is told so rather than working it out. A
-- child still running has not finished writing, so there is nothing to give
-- back yet, and a host that answered early would answer with part of the
-- output and no way of saying it was part.
rule CapturingAStreamMeansWaitingForTheChild {
    when: script_starts_a_program(run, program)
    requires: processes in run.interpreter.bounds.granted_services
    requires: program.standard_output = captured
        or program.standard_error = captured
        or program.standard_input = supplied_bytes

    ensures: program.waited_for = true
}

-- What comes back is the process number, and the exit code as well when the
-- child was waited for. A number that is not there is absent rather than
-- zero: a program that has not finished has no exit code, and zero is the
-- code for a program that finished perfectly.
rule AnUnwaitedProgramHasNoExitCodeYet {
    when: host_reports_a_started_program(run, result)
    requires: not result.was_waited_for

    ensures: result.exit_code = null
}

-- A file chooser's filters are pairs, and a list with an odd number of items
-- is refused before the dialog opens.
--
-- Before it opens is the whole point. A malformed list is the script's
-- mistake and nothing about it improves by asking a person first: an
-- operator shown a chooser, who picks a file, and is then told the call was
-- invalid has done work for nothing and cannot tell what went wrong.
rule AFilterListOfOddLengthIsRefusedBeforeTheDialogOpens {
    when: script_asks_the_operator_to_choose_a_file(run, request)
    requires: windows in run.interpreter.bounds.granted_services
    requires: not the_filter_items_pair_up(run)

    ensures:
        run.conclusion = raised
        no_window_was_shown(request)
}

-- Each item of the filter list is formed to text, so an item that is not a
-- string is not a mistake. A word, a file or a number becomes its own
-- spelling and goes to the chooser as that.
--
-- Formed rather than molded, so a file contributes its path and not a path
-- with a percent sign in front of it. Only the count is checked, which is
-- why the odd-length refusal is the only way a filter list can be wrong.
rule EachFilterItemIsFormedToText {
    when: script_asks_the_operator_to_choose_a_file(run, request)
    requires: windows in run.interpreter.bounds.granted_services

    ensures:
        for item in the_filter_items_written_by(run):
            request.filters.holds(formed(item))
}

-- A filter pattern the host cannot honour is applied as far as the host can
-- and otherwise ignored. The chooser still opens.
--
-- A decision, and it is about which of two disappointments to prefer. A
-- desktop chooser typically filters by extension and no further, so "*.r3"
-- works and "r3-*" does not. Refusing the call would stop a script for a
-- request the operator would not have minded, and would make a script's
-- portability depend on which patterns each host's chooser happens to
-- support. Showing everything is the lesser loss: the operator sees more
-- files than the script suggested and can still pick the right one.
--
-- What is not acceptable is silently choosing a different file. The filter
-- narrows what is offered and never what is answered, so whatever the
-- operator picks is what the script gets.
rule AFilterTheHostCannotHonourIsAppliedAsFarAsItGoes {
    when: script_asks_the_operator_to_choose_a_file(run, request)
    requires: windows in run.interpreter.bounds.granted_services
    requires: some_pattern_the_host_cannot_honour(request)

    ensures: the_chooser_is_shown(request)
}

-- A dialog the operator declined answers none. The three refusal reasons are
-- about the service and never about the answer, so a script can tell a
-- cancelled file chooser from a filesystem it was not granted.
rule ADeclinedDialogAnswersNone {
    when: script_asks_the_operator(run, request)
    requires: windows in run.interpreter.bounds.granted_services
    requires: operator_declined(request)
    ensures: WindowResult(value: none)
}

-- A host that granted the windows service and supplied nothing to put a
-- window on gets not_present rather than not_granted. The two are different
-- facts about the host and a script may act on either: the first can be
-- fixed by granting, the second cannot.
rule GrantedWithNoScreenIsNotPresent {
    when: script_asks_the_operator(run, request)
    requires: windows in run.interpreter.bounds.granted_services
    requires: run.interpreter.window_port = null
    ensures:
        run.conclusion = raised
        run.refusal_reason = not_present
        run.refused_service = windows
}

rule ScriptRunBegins {
    when: Run(interpreter, source_text)
    requires: interpreter.active_runs.count = 0
    ensures: ScriptRun.created(
        interpreter: interpreter,
        source_text: source_text,
        started_at: now,
        status: running,
        cancellation_requested: false
    )
}

-- The single place a run's outcome is decided, in precedence order.
-- Stopping beats finishing: a script that produced a value after its
-- deadline produced it too late to be wanted.
rule ScriptRunConcludes {
    when: run: ScriptRun.evaluation_settled
    requires: run.status = running

    ensures:
        if run.overdue:
            run.status = finished
            run.conclusion = timed_out
            run.result = timeout_error()
        else if run.cancellation_requested:
            run.status = finished
            run.conclusion = cancelled
            run.result = cancelled_error()
        else if evaluation_raised(run):
            run.status = finished
            run.conclusion = raised
            run.result = raised_error_of(run)
        else:
            run.status = finished
            run.conclusion = produced_a_value
            run.result = evaluation_result_of(run)
}

-- QUIT ends the run and nothing else. In a real REBOL it ends the process
-- too, because there the script IS the process; here a script is a guest
-- and the host is the one entitled to decide whether anything exits. A
-- guest that could take the host down with it is not embeddable, and a
-- host that had to guard against it could not run untrusted code at all.
--
-- It carries a value because REBOL's QUIT/RETURN does, and because a host
-- running a script to get an answer wants the answer rather than a bare
-- "it stopped".
rule QuitEndsTheRunWithoutEndingTheHost {
    when: run: ScriptRun.quit_requested

    requires: run.status = running

    ensures:
        run.status = finished
        run.conclusion = quit_early
        run.result = quit_value_of(run)

    @guidance
        -- Without /RETURN the value is none, matching REBOL. The
        -- refinement chooses the value; it does not choose whether there
        -- is one.
}

-- A run that passes its deadline is stopped whether or not it was going to
-- finish. Without this the bound is advice.
rule OverdueRunIsStopped {
    when: run: ScriptRun.overdue
    requires: run.status = running
    ensures: run.cancellation_requested = true
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Nothing a script does escapes as a host exception, including running out
-- of time. A host catching REBOL failures by catching Java exceptions would
-- be catching bugs in JEBOL as well, and could not tell them apart.
invariant EveryConclusionIsAValue {
    for run in ScriptRuns:
        run.status = finished implies run.result.datatype in {error}
            or run.conclusion in {produced_a_value, quit_early}
}

-- A finished run took no longer than its deadline allowed, give or take
-- however long the evaluator takes to notice. A bound that could be
-- exceeded without limit is not a bound.
invariant FinishedRunsRespectTheirDeadline {
    for run in ScriptRuns:
        run.conclusion = timed_out implies run.elapsed >= interpreter_limit_of(run)
}

-- An interpreter runs one script at a time. Concurrency comes from having
-- several interpreters, not from sharing one, because the value model has
-- no synchronisation in it and deliberately so.
invariant RunsDoNotOverlap {
    for run in ScriptRuns:
        run.status = running implies run.interpreter.active_runs.count = 1
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface Embedding {
    facing host: HostApplication

    provides:
        Run(host, interpreter, source_text)
        RunNext(host, interpreter, source_text)
        Cancel(host, interpreter)

    exposes:
        Conclusion
        Bounds
        HostService
        ServiceRefusal
        FileInformation
        WindowRequest
        ProgramInput
        ProgramOutput
        ProgramToStart
        ProgramResult
        FileChoiceRequest

    @guarantee AStartedProgramArrivesSplitWithItsThreeStreamsDecided
        -- A command crosses the boundary already separated into the program
        -- and its arguments, so the host never splits a line and never
        -- applies quoting rules of its own. A script that wants the system's
        -- own rules asks for the shell, and then the single entry is a
        -- command line the shell reads.
        --
        -- Each of the child's three streams is decided separately. Its input
        -- comes from the host's own, from bytes the script supplied, from a
        -- file, or from nothing at all. Each of its two outputs goes to the
        -- host's own, back to the interpreter, into a file, or nowhere.
        --
        -- Two of those choices are quiet traps. Closing a child's input is
        -- not the same as leaving it on the host's, because the first reads
        -- end of input at once and the second waits for someone who may
        -- never type. And a system that refused to start the program at all
        -- is not a program that ran and failed, so what comes back
        -- distinguishes a message from the system from an exit code.

    @guarantee TheHostSaysWhatItKnowsAboutAFileAndNoMore
        -- Six facts cross the boundary about one file: its name, whether it
        -- is a directory, its size, and when it was created, last read and
        -- last changed. That is the whole of what QUERY can answer, so a
        -- port offering more would be offering something no script can ask
        -- for.
        --
        -- A fact the host has not got is none for that field alone and the
        -- rest of the answer stands. A host that knows a file's size and
        -- not when it was last read is an ordinary host.

    @guarantee AHostGrantsServicesOneAtATime
        -- A script can reach the file system, the environment, another
        -- program, the operator, the clock, the network and the screen.
        -- Each is a separate grant and none is given by default.
        --
        -- One kind at a time and not one function at a time, because a
        -- host that lets a script read a file almost always lets it read
        -- every file it can see. A grant per function reads as finer
        -- control and gives none: the host cannot say which file, only
        -- which verb, and the verb was never the interesting half.
        --
        -- A script that asks for a service it has not been granted gets
        -- an error that names the service and says which of three things
        -- happened: the host has it and did not grant it, the host has no
        -- such service, or nothing can offer it. The last covers the four
        -- that exist to call code written in C. A script cannot tell an
        -- empty answer from a refused one, thus none of them may answer
        -- with silence.
        --
        -- The working directory belongs to the interpreter. A host runs
        -- many interpreters at once and each must be able to sit in a
        -- different directory, so changing one must not move another.

    @guarantee RunNextStopsAfterOneExpression
        -- RunNext evaluates the first expression of the source and hands
        -- back both its conclusion and the source still unread, so a host
        -- can walk a script an expression at a time. Everything else about
        -- it is Run: the same bounds, the same conclusions, the same
        -- promise that nothing escapes as a host exception.
        --
        -- Needed because "the value of this source" and "the value of its
        -- first expression" are different questions, and only Run answered
        -- either. A console answers the first for a line; a test harness
        -- reading `--assert all [...] a: none` needs the second, because
        -- the assertion is the ALL and the rest is ordinary code that
        -- still has to run.
        --
        -- Three attempts to build this beside the interpreter rather than
        -- inside it each lost ground: REDUCE fails wholesale when a later
        -- expression does, and a hand-assembled evaluator did not carry
        -- the same bounds or fresh-word handling. The seam belongs here.

    @guarantee NoFailureEscapesAsAHostException
        -- Every way a script can stop arrives as a conclusion and a value,
        -- including timing out and nesting too deep. A host never has to
        -- catch a throwable to find out that a script misbehaved.

    @guarantee BoundsAreEnforcedNotAdvertised
        -- A script that passes its deadline is stopped. A script that nests
        -- past its limit is stopped. Neither depends on the script being
        -- willing to cooperate.

    @guarantee AnInterpreterIsOwnedByOneThread
        -- Handing one to two threads is a mistake JEBOL does not defend
        -- against and does not make safe. Run several instead.

    @guidance
        -- Cancellation is cooperative: the evaluator checks between steps
        -- rather than being interrupted where it stands. Stopping a thread
        -- mid-mutation would leave series half-changed, and series are
        -- shared, so the damage would outlive the script.
}

actor HostApplication {
    identified_by: Interpreter where active_runs.count >= 0
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Does attaching a started program to the host's console differ observably from letting it inherit the host's streams, in a host that has no console? The C passes a flag and leaves the host to decide, so both readings are available: a console-less host could treat the request as ordinary inheritance, or it could refuse it and say the host has no console to attach to. The first keeps a script portable and gives the operator nothing; the second is honest and stops a script that would have worked. It matters for JEBOL because the common case is a server with no console at all, which is the opposite of the case the refinement was designed for."

open question "Does a file feeding a started program's input cross the boundary as a path for the host to open, or as bytes the interpreter read? The C passes the path and the host opens it, which is what this spec follows and why ProgramInput has four members rather than three. Reading it in the interpreter would leave the host with one way of being fed rather than two, at the cost of holding a whole file in memory and of the host losing the chance to stream it. Nothing a script can observe separates them, so this is a question about where the work sits rather than about behaviour."

open question "How often does the evaluator check whether it should stop? Every step is safest and slowest; every hundredth is cheaper and lets a tight loop overrun by a hundred steps. What is the right granularity, and should it be measured rather than guessed?"

open question "Is there a memory bound as well as a time bound? A script can allocate without nesting, so a depth limit does not stop it. Bounding heap per interpreter needs something the JVM does not offer directly."

open question "What does a host see of a script's progress while it runs? Nothing at all is simplest; a partial result or a progress callback would need the boundary to be crossed while the script is still running, which the one-thread rule forbids."
